    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Marketing</span> is the business activity that connects producers to consumers. It is much more than just selling — it encompasses all activities involved in identifying, anticipating, and satisfying customer needs profitably. Modern marketing is driven by the customer, not just the product.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Marketing</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Marketing can be defined as:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The process of identifying, anticipating, and satisfying customer needs and wants profitably</li>\n  <li>According to the <strong>American Marketing Association (AMA):</strong> 'The activity, set of institutions, and processes for creating, communicating, delivering, and exchanging offerings that have value for customers, clients, partners, and society at large'</li>\n  <li>Philip Kotler: 'Marketing is the social and managerial process by which individuals and groups obtain what they need and want through creating and exchanging products and value with others'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Nature of Marketing</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Customer-oriented:</strong> Focus is on satisfying customer needs, not just selling</li>\n  <li><strong>Goal-directed:</strong> Aimed at achieving business objectives (profit, market share, growth)</li>\n  <li><strong>Dynamic:</strong> Constantly changing with market conditions, technology, and consumer behaviour</li>\n  <li><strong>Integrated:</strong> Coordinates all business functions toward customer satisfaction</li>\n  <li><strong>Exchange-based:</strong> Marketing involves exchange — giving something of value to receive something of value</li>\n  <li><strong>Mutually beneficial:</strong> Both buyer and seller benefit from the exchange</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Scope of Marketing</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Marketing covers a wide range of activities:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Marketing Activity</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Market research</td><td>Gathering information about customers and markets</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Product development</td><td>Creating products that meet customer needs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Setting the right price for profitability and competitiveness</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Getting products to where customers can buy them</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Promotion</td><td>Communicating product benefits to target customers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Selling</td><td>The personal exchange process between buyer and seller</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>After-sales service</td><td>Maintaining customer satisfaction after purchase</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Key Marketing Terms</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Need:</strong> A state of felt deprivation (e.g., hunger, thirst)</li>\n  <li><strong>Want:</strong> Needs shaped by culture and individual personality (e.g., wanting pizza to satisfy hunger)</li>\n  <li><strong>Demand:</strong> Wants backed by buying power (ability and willingness to pay)</li>\n  <li><strong>Product:</strong> Anything offered to a market to satisfy a need or want</li>\n  <li><strong>Exchange:</strong> Giving something of value to receive something desired</li>\n  <li><strong>Market:</strong> All actual and potential buyers of a product</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The key distinction between needs, wants, and demand: NEED is fundamental (everyone needs food); WANT is how you prefer to satisfy that need (pizza, rice, etc.); DEMAND is a want backed by purchasing power. Marketing focuses on converting wants into demand by creating desirable, affordable products.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Marketing = identifying, anticipating, and satisfying customer needs profitably. Nature: customer-oriented, dynamic, exchange-based. Scope covers: research, product development, pricing, distribution, promotion, selling, after-sales. Key concepts: need (basic deprivation), want (need shaped by culture), demand (want + buying power), exchange (giving value to receive value).\n</div>\n  ",
