    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Over time, businesses have adopted different philosophies about how to approach their markets and customers. These philosophies are called <span class=\"learn-keyword\">marketing concepts</span> (or management orientations). There are five main concepts: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">production</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">product</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">selling</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">marketing</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">societal marketing</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Production Concept</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Assumes consumers prefer products that are widely available and inexpensive. Focus: produce in large quantities to reduce costs.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Orientation: <em>'If we make it, they will buy it'</em> — focus on production efficiency</li>\n  <li>Suitable when: demand exceeds supply; product is not differentiated</li>\n  <li>Limitation: ignores what customers actually want</li>\n  <li>Example: Henry Ford's Model T — 'any colour as long as it's black'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Product Concept</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Assumes consumers prefer products with the best quality, performance, and features. Focus: continuous product improvement.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Orientation: <em>'Build a better mousetrap and the world will beat a path to your door'</em></li>\n  <li>Limitation: 'marketing myopia' — over-focusing on product while ignoring customer needs (e.g., Kodak focused on film cameras while digital photography emerged)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Selling Concept</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Assumes consumers will not buy enough unless the firm undertakes aggressive selling and promotion efforts. Focus: push products onto customers.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Orientation: <em>'Sell what you make rather than make what you can sell'</em></li>\n  <li>Suitable for: unsought goods (insurance, encyclopaedias), overcapacity situations</li>\n  <li>Limitation: focuses on seller's needs rather than buyer's needs; may create short-term sales but long-term customer dissatisfaction</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Marketing Concept</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The key to achieving business goals is to be more effective than competitors at creating, delivering, and communicating superior customer value. Focus: understand and satisfy customer needs better than competitors.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Orientation: <em>'Find needs and fill them'</em> — start with the customer</li>\n  <li>Four pillars: target market, customer needs, integrated marketing, profitability</li>\n  <li>Contrast with selling concept: selling focuses on seller's needs; marketing on buyer's needs</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Societal Marketing Concept</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Extends the marketing concept by considering society's long-term well-being alongside customer and business interests.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Balances three interests: company profit + consumer wants + society's well-being</li>\n  <li>Considers environmental impact, social responsibility, and sustainability</li>\n  <li>Example: Toyota's hybrid cars, companies reducing plastic packaging, sustainable sourcing</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Focus</th><th>Key Phrase</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Production</td><td>Produce cheaply and widely</td><td>'Make it; they'll buy it'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Product</td><td>Best quality product</td><td>'Better product = more buyers'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Selling</td><td>Aggressive promotion</td><td>'Sell what you make'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Marketing</td><td>Customer needs</td><td>'Find needs and fill them'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Societal</td><td>Customer + society well-being</td><td>'Good for consumer AND society'</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The FIVE concepts in chronological evolution: Production → Product → Selling → Marketing → Societal. The modern approach is the MARKETING concept (customer-centred) and its evolution, SOCIETAL marketing (customer + society). The selling concept and marketing concept are often confused — remember: SELLING starts with the product; MARKETING starts with the customer.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Five marketing concepts: Production (produce cheaply, widely), Product (best quality), Selling (aggressive push), Marketing (understand and satisfy customer needs), Societal Marketing (customer + society well-being). Modern businesses use the marketing and societal concepts. Marketing myopia = over-focusing on product without seeing evolving customer needs.\n</div>\n  ",
