    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Products are at the heart of marketing — they are what businesses offer to satisfy customer needs. Products can be classified in many ways: by the type of buyer (consumer vs industrial), by tangibility (goods vs services), and by durability. Understanding product classification helps marketers develop appropriate strategies.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Consumer Goods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Consumer goods are products purchased by individuals and households for personal use (not for business or resale).</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Consumer goods are classified by shopping behaviour:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Characteristics</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Convenience goods</td><td>Bought frequently, routinely, with minimum effort; low price</td><td>Bread, newspapers, matches, soap, Indomie</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Shopping goods</td><td>Purchased less frequently; consumers compare price, quality, style before buying</td><td>Clothing, furniture, television sets, refrigerators</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Specialty goods</td><td>Unique features or brand identification; buyers are willing to make a special effort to obtain</td><td>Luxury cars (Mercedes), designer fashion, Rolex watches</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Unsought goods</td><td>Consumers are unaware of or don't normally think to buy; requires active promotion</td><td>Life insurance, encyclopaedias, funeral services</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Industrial Goods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Industrial goods (also called business goods or B2B goods) are purchased by organisations to use in production or business operations.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Raw materials</td><td>Natural products for processing</td><td>Cotton, iron ore, crude oil, timber</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Component parts</td><td>Enter final product directly</td><td>Tyres for cars, chips for computers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Capital equipment</td><td>Long-lasting machinery and facilities</td><td>Factory machines, generators, computers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Supplies and services</td><td>Supporting operations but not in final product</td><td>Office stationery, cleaning services, lubricants</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Key Differences: Consumer vs Industrial Goods</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Consumer Goods</th><th>Industrial Goods</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Buyer</td><td>Individuals/households</td><td>Organisations/businesses</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Personal use/consumption</td><td>Production/business operations</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Buying process</td><td>Often emotional, individual</td><td>Rational, formal purchasing process</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Quantity</td><td>Small quantities</td><td>Large quantities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Derived demand</td><td>Primary demand</td><td>Derived from consumer demand</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The same physical product can be consumer OR industrial depending on who buys it and for what purpose. Sugar bought by a household = consumer good. Sugar bought by a beverage company to make drinks = industrial good. Classification depends on PURPOSE and BUYER, not the product itself.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Consumer goods (for personal use): convenience (bread, soap), shopping (TV, clothes), specialty (luxury cars, Rolex), unsought (insurance). Industrial goods (for business/production): raw materials, component parts, capital equipment, supplies. Classification depends on buyer and purpose. Industrial demand is derived from consumer demand.\n</div>\n  ",
