    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Beyond the consumer/industrial distinction, products can also be classified by their <span class=\"learn-keyword\">durability</span> (how long they last) and by whether they are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">tangible goods</span> or <span class=\"learn-keyword\">intangible services</span>. These classifications affect how products are marketed, priced, distributed, and positioned.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Durable Goods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Durable goods are tangible products that last for a long time, typically survive many uses, and provide utility over an extended period.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Long useful life (years or decades)</li>\n  <li>Examples: cars, refrigerators, televisions, washing machines, buildings, furniture</li>\n  <li>Marketing implications: higher prices; personal selling important; strong after-sales service and warranties needed; bought infrequently</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Non-Durable Goods</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Non-durable goods are tangible products that are consumed quickly — in one or a few uses — and need frequent repurchase.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Short useful life (consumed quickly)</li>\n  <li>Examples: food, beverages, soap, newspapers, matches, petrol</li>\n  <li>Marketing implications: lower prices; widely distributed; heavy advertising to build brand preference; high-volume, high-frequency purchases</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Services</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Services are intangible activities or benefits that one party offers to another without resulting in ownership of anything physical.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Four unique characteristics of services (IHIP):</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Characteristic</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Marketing Implication</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Intangibility</td><td>Cannot be seen, touched, or tasted before purchase</td><td>Use tangible cues (certificates, uniforms, facilities) to signal quality</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Inseparability</td><td>Produced and consumed at the same time; provider and customer must interact</td><td>Service quality depends on provider-customer interaction</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Variability/Heterogeneity</td><td>Quality varies depending on who provides it and when</td><td>Train staff; use service standards to ensure consistency</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Perishability</td><td>Cannot be stored or inventoried; unused capacity is lost forever</td><td>Manage demand (reservations, off-peak pricing)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Examples: banking, education, healthcare, transportation, hairdressing, legal advice</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The four characteristics of services are captured by the acronym IHIP: Intangibility, Inseparability, Heterogeneity (Variability), Perishability. Services are the world's fastest-growing sector. Durable = lasts long; Non-durable = consumed quickly; Service = intangible, no ownership.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Durable goods (cars, fridges) = last long, high price, personal selling, warranties. Non-durable goods (food, soap) = consumed quickly, low price, mass advertising, wide distribution. Services (banking, education, healthcare) = intangible, inseparable from provider, variable quality, perishable (IHIP). Services require different marketing strategies from physical goods.\n</div>\n  ",
