    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">marketing mix</span> is the set of controllable marketing tools a firm uses to generate its desired response in the target market. The traditional marketing mix consists of the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">4Ps</span>: Product, Price, Place, and Promotion. These four elements must be carefully balanced and integrated to create a compelling offer for target customers.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Product</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Product is anything offered to the market to satisfy a need or want — physical goods, services, experiences, persons, places, organisations, or ideas.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Three levels of product:</strong> Core (the fundamental benefit), Actual (physical product features, quality, brand, packaging), Augmented (after-sales, warranties, delivery, installation)</li>\n  <li>Key decisions: design, features, quality, branding, packaging, labelling, warranties</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Price</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Price is the amount of money customers pay for a product. It is the only element of the mix that generates revenue (all others generate costs).</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Key decisions: pricing strategy (penetration, skimming, competitive), discounts, payment terms, credit</li>\n  <li>Price must reflect perceived value to the customer</li>\n  <li>Price signals quality — a very low price may suggest poor quality</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Place (Distribution)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Place refers to how a product is made available to the target market — the right product at the right place and right time.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Key decisions: distribution channels (direct, retail, wholesale), market coverage (intensive, selective, exclusive), logistics, transportation, warehousing</li>\n  <li>Channel types: manufacturer → consumer (direct); manufacturer → retailer → consumer; manufacturer → wholesaler → retailer → consumer</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Promotion</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Promotion is how the company communicates the product's value and benefits to target customers and persuades them to buy.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Promotion mix (tools): advertising, personal selling, sales promotion, public relations, direct marketing</li>\n  <li>Purpose: inform, persuade, remind</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>P</th><th>Questions Answered</th><th>Key Elements</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Product</td><td>What do we offer? What need does it satisfy?</td><td>Features, quality, design, brand, packaging, warranty</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Price</td><td>How much? What does it cost customers?</td><td>List price, discounts, payment terms, credit</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Place</td><td>Where and how do customers buy it?</td><td>Channels, coverage, logistics, transportation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Promotion</td><td>How do customers know about it? Why should they buy?</td><td>Advertising, selling, PR, sales promotion</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The 4Ps were developed by E. Jerome McCarthy (1960) and popularised by Philip Kotler. For SERVICES, 3 more Ps are added: People, Process, Physical Evidence (making 7Ps). Remember: PRICE is the only P that GENERATES REVENUE — all others involve costs. The 4Ps must be coordinated and integrated for maximum effectiveness.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Marketing Mix = 4Ps: Product (what you offer), Price (what customers pay — only revenue generator), Place (where/how it's available), Promotion (how you communicate value). For services: 7Ps adds People, Process, Physical Evidence. All 4Ps must be aligned with each other and with the target market. McCarthy developed 4Ps; Kotler popularised them.\n</div>\n  ",
