    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">marketing environment</span> consists of all forces outside and within the organisation that affect marketing management's ability to build and maintain relationships with target customers. Understanding the environment is essential — marketers cannot change it but must adapt to it.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Internal Marketing Environment</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Internal factors are within the organisation and can be controlled by management. They form the STRENGTHS and WEAKNESSES in SWOT analysis.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Company resources:</strong> Financial resources, human resources, production capacity</li>\n  <li><strong>Company culture:</strong> Values, attitudes, and management style within the organisation</li>\n  <li><strong>Departments:</strong> How well production, finance, HR, and marketing work together</li>\n  <li><strong>Marketing mix:</strong> The company's current product, price, place, and promotion decisions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. External Marketing Environment</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">External factors are outside the organisation and largely beyond its control. They form the OPPORTUNITIES and THREATS in SWOT analysis.</p>\n\n<h4>Micro-environment (Immediate External Environment)</h4>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Actors close to the company that directly affect its marketing:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Customers:</strong> Target markets whose needs must be understood</li>\n  <li><strong>Competitors:</strong> Other businesses offering similar products</li>\n  <li><strong>Suppliers:</strong> Provide inputs for production</li>\n  <li><strong>Intermediaries:</strong> Wholesalers, retailers, distributors who help sell</li>\n  <li><strong>Publics:</strong> Groups with interest in company (media, government, community)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Macro-environment (PESTEL/PESTLE)</h4>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Broader societal forces that affect all companies in the industry:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Political</td><td>Government stability, trade policies, regulations, taxes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Economic</td><td>Inflation, exchange rates, consumer income, GDP growth</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Social/Cultural</td><td>Demographics, lifestyle changes, cultural attitudes, education</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Technological</td><td>Internet, social media, AI, automation, new production methods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Environmental</td><td>Climate change, sustainability concerns, natural disasters</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Legal</td><td>Consumer protection laws, labour laws, advertising regulations</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> INTERNAL environment = controllable (within the company). EXTERNAL environment = largely uncontrollable (companies must adapt). Micro-environment = immediate actors (customers, competitors, suppliers). Macro-environment = broader societal forces (PESTEL). SWOT: Strengths/Weaknesses = internal; Opportunities/Threats = external.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Marketing environment: internal (controllable — company resources, culture, departments) and external. External = micro (customers, competitors, suppliers, intermediaries, publics) and macro (PESTEL: Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Environmental, Legal). SWOT: internal = S/W; external = O/T.\n</div>\n  ",
