    "topic": "Consumer Behaviour",
    "topicCode": "SS1-MKT-09",
    "module": "Buyer Behaviour",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Consumer behaviour</span> is the study of how individuals, groups, and organisations select, buy, use, and dispose of goods, services, ideas, or experiences to satisfy their needs and wants. Understanding consumer behaviour helps marketers create effective strategies that resonate with target customers.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Consumer Behaviour</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Consumer behaviour refers to the actions, decisions, and mental processes involved when individuals buy, use, and dispose of products. It answers: Who buys? What do they buy? Why do they buy? When, where, and how do they buy?</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Factors Influencing Consumer Behaviour</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor Type</th><th>Sub-factors</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Cultural Factors</td><td>Culture, subculture, social class</td><td>Yoruba preference for certain foods; social class affecting car choice</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Social Factors</td><td>Reference groups, family, roles and status</td><td>Friends influencing phone brand choice; family roles in house purchase</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Personal Factors</td><td>Age, occupation, economic circumstances, lifestyle, personality</td><td>Young adults buying smartphones; executives buying luxury cars</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Psychological Factors</td><td>Motivation, perception, learning, beliefs and attitudes</td><td>Maslow's hierarchy; brand perception; past experience with a product</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The Consumer Decision-Making Process</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Consumers go through five stages when making purchase decisions:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>1. Problem recognition:</strong> Consumer realises a need or problem (e.g., phone is broken)</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Information search:</strong> Consumer seeks information about solutions (ask friends, search online, visit stores)</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Evaluation of alternatives:</strong> Compare options using criteria (price, quality, brand, features)</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Purchase decision:</strong> Choose and buy the preferred option</li>\n  <li><strong>5. Post-purchase behaviour:</strong> Feel satisfied or have buyer's remorse; influences future buying and recommendations</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Abraham Maslow's hierarchy explains consumer motivation as a ladder of needs from basic to aspirational:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Physiological (food, water, shelter) → Safety (security, stability) → Social/Belonging (love, relationships) → Esteem (status, recognition) → Self-actualisation (personal fulfilment)</li>\n  <li>Lower needs must be largely satisfied before higher needs motivate behaviour</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The four types of factors influencing consumer behaviour: Cultural, Social, Personal, and Psychological (CSPП). The five stages of the consumer decision process: Problem Recognition → Information Search → Evaluation → Purchase → Post-purchase. Post-purchase cognitive dissonance (buyer's remorse) is important — marketers need to reassure buyers after purchase.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Consumer behaviour = how individuals buy, use, and dispose of products. Influencing factors: Cultural (culture, subculture, social class), Social (reference groups, family), Personal (age, income, lifestyle), Psychological (motivation, perception, learning, attitudes). Decision process: 5 stages (Problem → Search → Evaluation → Purchase → Post-purchase). Maslow's needs hierarchy explains consumer motivation.\n</div>\n  ",
