    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Organisational buying behaviour</span> (also called business buying or B2B buying) refers to how formal organisations — businesses, government agencies, and institutions — make purchase decisions for goods and services needed to operate, produce, or resell. It differs significantly from individual consumer behaviour.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Organisational Buying</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Organisational buying is the decision-making process by which formal organisations establish a need for purchased products and services, and identify, evaluate, and choose among alternative brands and suppliers.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Features of Organisational Buying</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Larger purchases:</strong> Greater volume and value per transaction than consumer purchases</li>\n  <li><strong>Rational/professional decision-making:</strong> Based on technical specifications, price, and performance — not emotion</li>\n  <li><strong>Multiple decision-makers (Buying Centre):</strong> Several people involved — users, influencers, buyers, deciders, gatekeepers</li>\n  <li><strong>Formal purchasing process:</strong> Requisitions, specifications, tendering, contracts</li>\n  <li><strong>Long-term supplier relationships:</strong> Organisations often build lasting supplier partnerships</li>\n  <li><strong>Derived demand:</strong> Demand depends on consumer demand</li>\n  <li><strong>Inelastic demand:</strong> Price changes have less effect (organisations still need key inputs)</li>\n  <li><strong>Geographically concentrated buyers:</strong> Industrial buyers clustered in certain areas</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Differences: Organisational vs Consumer Buying</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Organisational (B2B)</th><th>Consumer (B2C)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Production, operations, resale</td><td>Personal use</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Buyers</td><td>Professional purchasing staff</td><td>Individual consumers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Decision process</td><td>Formal, structured, lengthy</td><td>Often quick, informal</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Decision makers</td><td>Multiple (buying centre)</td><td>Usually one or few</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Volume</td><td>Large quantities</td><td>Small quantities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Motivation</td><td>Rational (ROI, specifications)</td><td>Mix of rational and emotional</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Supplier relationship</td><td>Long-term partnerships</td><td>Transactional, brand loyalty</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. The Buying Centre (Decision Making Unit)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Users:</strong> Actually use the product being purchased</li>\n  <li><strong>Influencers:</strong> Provide technical specifications or recommendations</li>\n  <li><strong>Buyers:</strong> Handle the actual purchasing process</li>\n  <li><strong>Deciders:</strong> Have formal authority to approve the purchase</li>\n  <li><strong>Gatekeepers:</strong> Control information flow (secretaries, assistants)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The Buying Centre (DMU = Decision Making Unit) is the key concept for organisational buying. Key differences from consumer buying: more rational, larger purchases, formal process, multiple decision-makers, long-term relationships. Industrial demand is DERIVED (depends on consumer demand) and INELASTIC (less sensitive to price changes).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Organisational buying = formal organisations purchasing for operations/production/resale. Features: large purchases, rational decisions, formal process, multiple decision-makers (buying centre), derived/inelastic demand, long-term relationships. Buying Centre roles: users, influencers, buyers, deciders, gatekeepers. More rational and formal than consumer buying.\n</div>\n  ",
