    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Advertising</span> is any paid, non-personal form of communication about a product, service, or organisation by an identified sponsor, through mass media. It is the most visible element of the promotion mix and one of the most powerful tools for building brand awareness and influencing consumer attitudes.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Advertising</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Advertising is paid, non-personal communication from an identified sponsor delivered to a mass audience through various media (television, radio, newspapers, billboards, internet) to inform, persuade, or remind target customers about a product, service, or organisation.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Advertising</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Informative advertising</td><td>Educate consumers about new products, features, or prices</td><td>Launching a new phone model — describing features</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Persuasive advertising</td><td>Build brand preference; persuade consumers to buy</td><td>Pepsi vs Coca-Cola comparative ads</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reminder advertising</td><td>Keep the brand top-of-mind for existing customers</td><td>Coca-Cola holiday ads, insurance renewal reminders</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Institutional/Corporate advertising</td><td>Build company image and goodwill — not selling a specific product</td><td>MTN's 'Y'ello' CSR campaigns</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Comparative advertising</td><td>Directly compares the product with competitors</td><td>Pepsi challenge ads against Coca-Cola</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Importance of Advertising</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Creates awareness of new products and brands</li>\n  <li>Informs consumers about product features, benefits, and prices</li>\n  <li>Builds brand image and differentiation</li>\n  <li>Stimulates demand and drives sales</li>\n  <li>Helps launch new products</li>\n  <li>Supports salespeople by pre-selling the product</li>\n  <li>Funds mass media (TV, radio, newspapers)</li>\n  <li>Promotes competition and consumer choice</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Objectives of Advertising (AIDA Model)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Advertising aims to move consumers through the AIDA hierarchy:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>A</strong>ttention — attract the consumer's notice</li>\n  <li><strong>I</strong>nterest — stimulate interest in the product</li>\n  <li><strong>D</strong>esire — create desire/want for the product</li>\n  <li><strong>A</strong>ction — motivate purchase</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key features of advertising: PAID (advertiser pays for space), NON-PERSONAL (mass communication, not one-to-one), IDENTIFIED SPONSOR (we know who is advertising), MASS AUDIENCE (reaches many simultaneously). Advertising is DIFFERENT from publicity (which is FREE media coverage) and personal selling (which is personal, one-to-one).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Advertising = paid, non-personal mass communication by identified sponsor. Types: informative (new product info), persuasive (brand preference), reminder (keep brand top-of-mind), institutional (company image), comparative. Importance: awareness, demand stimulation, brand building, supports sales. AIDA: Attention → Interest → Desire → Action.\n</div>\n  ",
