    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Advertising reaches consumers through various <span class=\"learn-keyword\">media channels</span>. Each medium has unique advantages and disadvantages. Effective advertisers choose media that best reach their target audience at the right cost, with the appropriate impact.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Media of Advertising</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Medium</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Television</td><td>Combines sight, sound, motion; wide reach; high impact; demonstrates products</td><td>Very expensive; short exposure; ad skipping; cluttered</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Radio</td><td>Wide reach; affordable; mobile audience; frequency; local targeting</td><td>Audio only (no visual); short exposure; less attention</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Newspapers</td><td>Wide readership; credibility; flexibility; can provide detail; local targeting</td><td>Short lifespan; declining readership; poor colour quality</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Magazines</td><td>High-quality colour; specific audience targeting; long shelf life; prestige</td><td>High cost; less timely; long lead time</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Outdoor/Billboards</td><td>High visibility; 24/7 exposure; geographic targeting; low cost per exposure</td><td>Limited message space; brief exposure time; fixed location</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Internet/Digital</td><td>Precise targeting; interactive; measurable; global reach; cost-effective; personalisation</td><td>Ad blocking; digital divide; security/privacy concerns; overwhelming clutter</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Direct mail</td><td>Highly targeted; personalised; measurable</td><td>Seen as junk mail; high cost per contact; poor response rate</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Factors Influencing Media Choice</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Target audience:</strong> Where do they consume media? (Youth → social media; elderly → TV/radio)</li>\n  <li><strong>Budget:</strong> TV is expensive; digital can be affordable</li>\n  <li><strong>Product type:</strong> TV best for demonstrations; radio for quick reminders</li>\n  <li><strong>Advertising objectives:</strong> Brand awareness (TV), targeted response (digital)</li>\n  <li><strong>Reach vs frequency:</strong> Need to reach many people once vs fewer people many times</li>\n  <li><strong>Geographic coverage:</strong> National vs local campaign</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> TV = highest IMPACT but most EXPENSIVE. Radio = most MOBILE (people listen while driving). Digital/Internet = most MEASURABLE and most TARGETED. Billboards = 24/7 EXPOSURE but limited message. Newspapers = most DETAILED information. In exams, always match media choice to the TARGET AUDIENCE and product type.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Advertising media: TV (high impact, expensive), radio (mobile, affordable), newspapers (credible, detailed), magazines (targeted, quality), outdoor/billboards (24/7, brief), digital (targeted, measurable, interactive), direct mail (personalised). Media choice depends on: target audience, budget, product type, objectives, reach vs frequency, geographic coverage.\n</div>\n  ",
