    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Promotion</span> is one of the 4Ps of the marketing mix — it encompasses all the activities businesses use to communicate the value of their products and persuade target customers to buy. The full set of promotional tools is called the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">promotion mix</span> or marketing communications mix.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Promotion</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Promotion refers to all the methods and activities used by a business to communicate with potential and existing customers about its products or services, with the aim of informing, persuading, and reminding them to make purchasing decisions.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types/Tools of Promotion (The Promotion Mix)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Advertising</td><td>Paid, non-personal mass communication</td><td>TV ads, radio jingles, billboards, online ads</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Personal Selling</td><td>One-to-one direct communication by salespeople</td><td>Sales reps visiting retailers, door-to-door selling</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sales Promotion</td><td>Short-term incentives to stimulate immediate purchase</td><td>Discounts, coupons, free samples, buy-one-get-one</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Public Relations (PR)</td><td>Building goodwill and managing company image</td><td>Press releases, sponsorships, CSR activities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Direct Marketing</td><td>Direct communication with specific individuals</td><td>Email marketing, SMS campaigns, direct mail</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Purposes of Promotion</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Inform:</strong> Educate consumers about new products, features, availability</li>\n  <li><strong>Persuade:</strong> Convince consumers to choose this product over competitors</li>\n  <li><strong>Remind:</strong> Keep existing customers aware and loyal</li>\n  <li><strong>Build brand image:</strong> Create and maintain a positive brand identity</li>\n  <li><strong>Stimulate demand:</strong> Generate immediate purchases and trials</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Factors Determining the Promotion Mix</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Type of product:</strong> Consumer goods → advertising; Industrial goods → personal selling</li>\n  <li><strong>Stage of product life cycle:</strong> Introduction → heavy advertising; Maturity → sales promotion</li>\n  <li><strong>Budget:</strong> Small budget → social media and PR; Large budget → TV and personal selling</li>\n  <li><strong>Target market:</strong> Consumer market → mass media; Business market → personal selling</li>\n  <li><strong>Push vs Pull strategy:</strong> Push (target intermediaries) vs Pull (target end consumers)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The FIVE promotion mix tools: Advertising, Personal Selling, Sales Promotion, Public Relations, Direct Marketing. Sales promotion = SHORT-TERM incentives. Personal selling = most EXPENSIVE per contact but most EFFECTIVE for complex products. PR = builds goodwill; not paid media. Promotion's purposes: INFORM, PERSUADE, REMIND.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Promotion = all communication activities to inform, persuade, and remind customers. Five promotion mix tools: advertising (mass, paid), personal selling (one-to-one), sales promotion (short-term incentives), PR (image/goodwill), direct marketing (targeted individual communication). Mix choice depends on product type, life cycle stage, budget, and target market.\n</div>\n  ",
