    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Merchandising</span> refers to all activities involved in making products available, visible, and attractive at the point of sale to encourage purchase. It is the 'silent salesperson' — the way products are displayed, presented, and positioned in-store significantly influences buying decisions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Merchandising</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Merchandising encompasses the activities and strategies used to promote products at the point of sale — including product display, shelf positioning, in-store promotions, packaging, and product mix decisions — to attract customers and maximise sales.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Importance of Merchandising</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Increases product visibility at the point of purchase</li>\n  <li>Stimulates impulse buying — customers buy things they didn't plan to</li>\n  <li>Reinforces brand image at the most critical decision point</li>\n  <li>Maximises sales per unit of floor/shelf space</li>\n  <li>Creates an appealing shopping experience</li>\n  <li>Helps introduce new products by placing them prominently</li>\n  <li>Supports advertising by ensuring advertised products are available and visible</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Merchandising Techniques</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Product display and shelf positioning</td><td>Eye-level shelving maximises visibility; end-of-aisle displays attract attention</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Point-of-sale (POS) materials</td><td>In-store signs, banners, price tags, floor stickers highlighting products</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Window display</td><td>Attractive shop window arrangements to draw customers in</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Product bundling/cross-selling</td><td>Placing related products together to encourage multiple purchases</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Impulse placement</td><td>Placing items near checkout counter for last-minute purchases</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Planogramming</td><td>Systematic shelf layout planning to optimise product placement</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Lighting and ambiance</td><td>Store atmosphere design to influence mood and buying behaviour</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Merchandising = the 'silent salesperson' at the point of sale. Key principle: EYE-LEVEL IS BUY-LEVEL (products placed at eye level sell best). IMPULSE PURCHASES are driven by checkout counter placement. Merchandising stimulates UNPLANNED purchases. It supports all other marketing activities at the final decision point.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Merchandising = making products visible, available, and attractive at point of sale. Importance: product visibility, impulse buying, brand reinforcement, max sales per space. Techniques: shelf positioning, POS materials, window displays, product bundling, impulse placement near checkout, planogramming, store atmosphere. 'Eye-level is buy-level.'\n</div>\n  ",
