    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic extends merchandising to cover <span class=\"learn-keyword\">trade marketing</span> — the relationship between manufacturers and retailers — and the emerging role of <span class=\"learn-keyword\">e-commerce merchandising</span>. It also covers stock management and retail performance measures.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Trade Marketing</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Trade marketing is the set of marketing activities directed at the trade (retailers, wholesalers, distributors) rather than at end consumers. Manufacturers use trade marketing to ensure their products get into and are well-positioned within retail stores.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Objectives: secure retail listing, gain shelf space, achieve display quality, build trade relationships</li>\n  <li>Tools: trade promotions, trade advertising, in-store support (field merchandisers), category management</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. E-Commerce Merchandising</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Online retail requires digital equivalents of physical merchandising:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Physical Store</th><th>Online Equivalent</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Shelf positioning</td><td>Search ranking; homepage featuring</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Window display</td><td>Website homepage banner/hero image</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Product display</td><td>Product photography, descriptions, reviews</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cross-merchandising</td><td>'Customers also bought' recommendations</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Store atmosphere</td><td>Website design, speed, user experience</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Checkout impulse buys</td><td>Add-to-cart upsell/cross-sell prompts</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Stock Management in Merchandising</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Out-of-stock:</strong> Products not available → lost sales and customer dissatisfaction</li>\n  <li><strong>Overstocking:</strong> Too much inventory → tied-up capital, storage costs, potential waste</li>\n  <li><strong>Minimum stock level:</strong> Safety buffer to prevent stockouts</li>\n  <li><strong>Reorder level:</strong> Point at which new stock should be ordered</li>\n  <li><strong>Stocktaking:</strong> Regular physical counting of inventory</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Trade marketing = manufacturer marketing to retailers (B2B within the supply chain). E-commerce has digital equivalents for every physical merchandising technique. Stock management: avoid STOCKOUTS (lost sales) AND OVERSTOCKING (wasted capital). Key metric: gross margin return on inventory investment (GMROI).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Trade marketing: manufacturer activities targeting retailers (listing, shelf space, display). E-commerce merchandising: digital equivalents (search ranking = shelf position; homepage = window). Stock management: avoid stockouts (lost sales) and overstocking (wasted capital). Good merchandising at all levels maximises product availability and visibility.\n</div>\n  ",
