    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This final merchandising topic covers the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">product life cycle</span> and how it affects merchandising strategy, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">brand management</span> in merchandising, and the role of <span class=\"learn-keyword\">packaging</span> as a merchandising tool.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Product Life Cycle (PLC) and Merchandising</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The Product Life Cycle has four stages — each requiring different merchandising strategies:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Sales</th><th>Merchandising Strategy</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Introduction</td><td>Low, growing slowly</td><td>Prominent placement, sampling, POS support; secure retail listing</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Growth</td><td>Rapidly rising</td><td>Expand distribution; increase facings; maximise shelf space</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Maturity</td><td>Peak, slowing</td><td>Maintain shelf space; compete with promotions; category management</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Decline</td><td>Falling</td><td>Reduce facings; clear stock; consider delisting; end promotional support</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Packaging as a Merchandising Tool</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Packaging is the 'silent salesperson' on the shelf — it must attract attention, communicate benefits, and motivate purchase without any verbal communication.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Attention-attracting:</strong> Distinctive shape, colour, or design that stands out on shelf</li>\n  <li><strong>Communication:</strong> Brand name, key benefits, usage instructions, ingredients</li>\n  <li><strong>Protection:</strong> Protects product during distribution and on shelf</li>\n  <li><strong>Convenience:</strong> Easy to open, carry, store, and use</li>\n  <li><strong>Eco-friendly:</strong> Sustainable packaging appeals to environmentally conscious consumers</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Private Label (Own-Brand) Merchandising</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Private label products are manufactured by a third party but sold under the retailer's own brand. Merchandising implications:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Retailers give own-brand products prime shelf positions</li>\n  <li>Often priced below manufacturer brands to offer value</li>\n  <li>Higher profit margins for retailers than national brands</li>\n  <li>Examples: Shoprite brand products, Spar branded goods</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Product Life Cycle: Introduction (new — need to build presence), Growth (growing — expand), Maturity (peak — maintain), Decline (falling — reduce/delist). Packaging = the 'silent salesperson' on shelf. Private labels (own-brands) give retailers higher margins and prime shelf positions.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> PLC affects merchandising: introduction (secure listing, prominent placement), growth (expand distribution and facings), maturity (maintain shelf, promotions), decline (reduce, delist). Packaging: silent salesperson (attracts attention, communicates, protects, convenient). Private labels: retailer's own brand, higher margins, given prime positioning.\n</div>\n  ",
