    "topic": "Distribution",
    "topicCode": "SS1-MKT-24",
    "module": "Distribution",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Distribution</span> (the 'Place' P in the marketing mix) is the process of making products available to target customers at the right place, at the right time, and in the right quantities. An efficient distribution system is essential for marketing success — even the best product fails if customers cannot access it.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Distribution</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Distribution refers to all activities involved in moving products from producers to final consumers, including transportation, warehousing, inventory management, order processing, and channel management.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Distribution Channels</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A distribution channel is the network of organisations (intermediaries) through which a product passes from the manufacturer to the end consumer.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Channel Level</th><th>Flow</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Zero-level (direct)</td><td>Manufacturer → Consumer</td><td>Dell computers direct, farm-to-consumer, e-commerce</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>One-level</td><td>Manufacturer → Retailer → Consumer</td><td>Clothing manufacturer to fashion retailer</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Two-level</td><td>Manufacturer → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer</td><td>FMCG goods (most common)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Three-level</td><td>Manufacturer → Agent → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer</td><td>International distribution</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Intermediaries and Their Functions</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Wholesalers:</strong> Buy in bulk from manufacturers, break bulk, store, and sell to retailers</li>\n  <li><strong>Retailers:</strong> Sell directly to final consumers in small quantities</li>\n  <li><strong>Agents/Brokers:</strong> Facilitate transactions between buyer and seller without taking ownership</li>\n  <li><strong>Distributors:</strong> Take ownership and market products in a defined territory</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Distribution Intensity</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Products</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Intensive</td><td>Maximum number of outlets — available everywhere</td><td>Convenience goods: Coca-Cola, matches, soap</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Selective</td><td>Chosen limited number of suitable outlets</td><td>Shopping goods: TVs, appliances</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Exclusive</td><td>One or very few outlets per geographic area</td><td>Luxury goods: Mercedes dealerships</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Importance of Distribution</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Creates place utility — products available where needed</li>\n  <li>Creates time utility — products available when needed</li>\n  <li>Reduces transaction costs through specialised intermediaries</li>\n  <li>Provides market information flow back to manufacturers</li>\n  <li>Makes products accessible to large numbers of consumers</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Distribution intensity: INTENSIVE = everywhere (convenience goods); SELECTIVE = chosen outlets (shopping goods); EXCLUSIVE = one dealer (luxury goods). Channel levels: zero-level = direct (no intermediaries); one-level = one intermediary; two-level = two intermediaries (most common for FMCG). Intermediaries add value by breaking bulk, storing, transporting, and providing market access.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Distribution = moving products from producer to consumer. Channels: zero-level (direct), one-level (retailer), two-level (wholesaler+retailer), three-level (agent+wholesaler+retailer). Intermediaries: wholesalers, retailers, agents, distributors. Distribution intensity: intensive (everywhere), selective (chosen), exclusive (one dealer). Creates place and time utility.\n</div>\n  ",
